#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082957 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/id #uuid "ec516fb4-a497-579c-b19d-ebc0a081eb58" :question/math-academy-id "e-5822" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u} =\\dfrac{5}{u^2 +u}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u}  =\\dfrac{5}{u(u+1)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\big( 3u, \\:u+1, \\: u(u+1) \\big) = 3u(u+1).\n$$\n\n- The values $u=-1$ and $u=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{u+1}-\\frac{1}{3u} & =\\frac{5}{u(u+1)} \\\\ 3u(u+1)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{u+1}-\\frac{1}{3u}) & =3u(u+1)\\cdot \\frac{5}{u(u+1)} \\\\ \\frac{9u(u+1)}{u+1}-\\frac{3u(u+1)}{3u} & =\\frac{15u(u+1)}{u(u+1)} \\\\ \\frac{9u\\cdot \\cancel{(u+1)}}{\\cancel{u+1}}-\\frac{\\cancel{3u}\\cdot (u+1)}{\\cancel{3u}} & =\\frac{15\\cdot \\cancel{u(u+1)}}{\\cancel{u(u+1)}} \\\\ 9u-(u+1) & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $u,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9u-(u+1) & =15 \\\\ 9u-u-1 & =15 \\\\ 8u & =16 \\\\ u & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\neq -1$ and $2\\neq 0,$ we conclude that $u=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $u=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082958 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/id #uuid "a578b895-ea0e-5d83-aa1d-04a2fe15f332" :question/math-academy-id "e-5824" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2}  = \\dfrac{2}{k^2-5k+6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2}  = \\dfrac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\big( k-2, k-3, (k-2)(k-3) \\big) = (k-2)(k-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $k=2$ and $k=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal to zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{k-3}-\\frac{2}{k-2} & =\\frac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)} \\\\ (k-2)(k-3)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{k-3}-\\frac{2}{k-2}) & =(k-2)(k-3)\\cdot \\frac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(k-2)(k-3)}{k-3}-\\frac{2(k-2)(k-3)}{k-2} & =\\frac{2(k-2)(k-3)}{(k-2)(k-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(k-2)\\cdot \\cancel{(k-3)}}{\\cancel{k-3}}-\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(k-2)}\\cdot (k-3)}{\\cancel{k-2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(k-2)(k-3)}}{\\cancel{(k-2)(k-3)}} \\\\ 3(k-2)-2(k-3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(k-2)-2(k-3) & =2 \\\\ 3k-6-2k+6 & =2 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $k=2$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083049 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "3384b27a-d977-5421-800e-64a3581b939d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9367" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{4} {x - 2} - \\dfrac{8} {x(x - 2)} = \\dfrac {3} {x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\big(x-2,\\: x(x-2),\\: x \\big) = x(x-2).\n$$\n\n- The values $x = 0$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{8}{x(x-2)} & =\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ x(x-2)\\cdot (\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{8}{x(x-2)}) & =x(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{3}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4x(x-2)}{x-2}-\\frac{8x(x-2)}{x(x-2)} & =\\frac{3x(x-2)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4x\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x-2}}-\\frac{8\\cancel{x(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x(x-2)}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{x}(x-2)}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\ 4x-8 & =3(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-8 & =3(x-2) \\\\ 4x-8 & =3x-6 \\\\ 4x-3x & =-6+8 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=2$ is not a valid solution, we conclude that our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e24be98-495c-5e12-a0e5-9c0e55900de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079773} {:answer/id #uuid "0179f2a9-e06d-5a25-a177-8d422aa158bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079774} {:answer/id #uuid "1b1783f4-d0c7-576b-9a15-b0f510954705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079775} {:answer/id #uuid "598418ac-6636-5dc1-90c1-451df7b1de18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079776} {:answer/id #uuid "746d232f-e963-5830-98de-ae92514ea094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "746d232f-e963-5830-98de-ae92514ea094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079777} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3cab350-0ed4-5fac-958c-5cd245c3ff1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9db8c64-2d0d-5a64-aada-e217c949b881" :question/math-academy-id "q-117631" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y}=\\frac{5}{2{y}^{2}+10y}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y}=\\frac{5}{2y(y+5)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(y+5,\\,2y,\\,2y(y+5))=2y(y+5).\n$$\n\n- The values $y=0$ and $y=-5$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y} & =\\frac{5}{2y(y+5)} \\\\ 2y(y+5)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y}) & =2y(y+5)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2y(y+5)} \\\\ \\frac{6y(y+5)}{y+5}+\\frac{2y(y+5)}{2y} & =\\frac{5\\cdot 2y(y+5)}{2y(y+5)} \\\\ \\frac{6y\\cancel{(y+5)}}{\\cancel{y+5}}+\\frac{\\cancel{2y}(y+5)}{\\cancel{2y}} & =\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{2y(y+5)}}{\\cancel{2y(y+5)}} \\\\ 6y+(y+5) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $y,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y+(y+5) & =5 \\\\ 7y+5 & =5 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $y=0$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016ef3cc-7d6e-56c4-8b26-7ce1164c6dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263432} {:answer/id #uuid "54a0baeb-d794-51da-83b9-446a4b0369b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263433} {:answer/id #uuid "408557e6-173c-50ce-87df-15446b7c4752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263434} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2cdf5f-d77a-5ff4-8a1e-87d171e12c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263435} {:answer/id #uuid "a198d35e-c8a2-541d-ab6f-5ee29a4dbd76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "016ef3cc-7d6e-56c4-8b26-7ce1164c6dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263432} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9599e57-b1c0-5efe-9915-6354bc652808" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "add7ad15-a989-5880-8090-8af6c15fef69" :question/math-academy-id "q-117682" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}=\\frac{8}{{V}^{2}-16}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}=\\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2(V+4),\\,V-4,\\,(V+4)(V-4))=2(V+4)(V-4).\n$$\n\n- The values $V=-4$ and $V=4$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4} & =\\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ 2(V+4)(V-4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}) & =2(V+4)(V-4)\\cdot \\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ \\frac{2(V+4)(V-4)}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{2(V+4)(V-4)}{V-4} & =\\frac{16(V+4)(V-4)}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2(V+4)}(V-4)}{\\cancel{2(V+4)}}+\\frac{2(V+4)\\cancel{(V-4)}}{\\cancel{V-4}} & =\\frac{16\\cancel{(V+4)(V-4)}}{\\cancel{(V+4)(V-4)}} \\\\ (V-4)+2(V+4) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(V-4)+2(V+4) & =16 \\\\ V-4+2V+8 & =16 \\\\ 3V & =12 \\\\ V & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $V=4$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1994042f-fdaa-5194-9330-5f50cb8e61a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297832} {:answer/id #uuid "14a3b11a-5be3-57d9-bb5b-fda637e3aa03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297833} {:answer/id #uuid "62473451-942f-5098-b544-fc42e2e35dbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297834} {:answer/id #uuid "58fb4430-a757-5f9b-8511-4076eceea268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297835} {:answer/id #uuid "60b2c9d7-fbd8-500c-beb5-d3f551d495bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1994042f-fdaa-5194-9330-5f50cb8e61a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297832} :answer-field/id #uuid "1223711f-0647-501c-a736-a04981473aeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa8f9db1-a194-5257-abcf-d7a9a1fa6588" :question/math-academy-id "q-117687" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}=\\frac{3}{{m}^{2}+m-12}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}=\\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m+4,\\,m-3,\\,(m+4)(m-3))=(m+4)(m-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $m=-4$ and $m=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3} & =\\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ (m+4)(m-3)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}) & =(m+4)(m-3)\\cdot \\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(m+4)(m-3)}{m+4}+\\frac{(m+4)(m-3)}{m-3} & =\\frac{3(m+4)(m-3)}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3\\cancel{(m+4)}(m-3)}{\\cancel{m+4}}+\\frac{(m+4)\\cancel{(m-3)}}{\\cancel{m-3}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{(m+4)(m-3)}}{\\cancel{(m+4)(m-3)}} \\\\ 3(m-3)+(m+4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(m-3)+(m+4) & =3 \\\\ 4m-5 & =3 \\\\ 4m & =8 \\\\ m & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\ne -4$ and $2\\ne 3,$ we conclude that $m=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $m=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122ef195-3efa-5e3e-abcb-71f1ec0c2cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297839} {:answer/id #uuid "01e5c475-9a3a-54d5-896e-8f77b4500aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297840} {:answer/id #uuid "17d5f93f-aefb-573e-9a95-f0224c689f91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297841} {:answer/id #uuid "e2550591-2c28-5ce9-bb3b-772979f303ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297842} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef0f39e-d394-50c0-9101-307b68c61340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2550591-2c28-5ce9-bb3b-772979f303ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297842} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ba1fedc-1332-5481-9e0a-b8e832baed58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a5ccbb7-a0cc-5e63-9faa-a0929882fb83" :question/math-academy-id "q-118123" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{{z}^{2}-z}=\\frac{1}{2z}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)}=\\frac{1}{2z}.\n$$\n\nWe make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(z-1,\\,2z,\\,z(z-1))=2z(z-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $z=0$ and $z=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{1}{2z} \\\\ 2z(z-1)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)}) & =2z(z-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2z} \\\\ \\frac{10z(z-1)}{z-1}-\\frac{10z(z-1)}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{2z(z-1)}{2z} \\\\ \\frac{10z\\cdot \\cancel{(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z-1}}-\\frac{10\\cdot \\cancel{z(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z(z-1)}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2z}\\cdot (z-1)}{\\cancel{2z}} \\\\ 10z-10 & =z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $z,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10z-10 & =z-1 \\\\ 9z & =9 \\\\ z & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $z=1$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0ae6c7b-12d4-5d78-999d-9d63cf99a263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297846} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1fed12-1d37-58f5-b456-a6b89e37491b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297847} {:answer/id #uuid "898c2ff9-57bf-53e8-98d4-f8db6ce3055d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297848} {:answer/id #uuid "11f57e12-50e7-5732-befb-be396cc29697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297849} {:answer/id #uuid "896a1ba5-4dd8-5021-8c7b-37cd8097a865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "898c2ff9-57bf-53e8-98d4-f8db6ce3055d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297848} :answer-field/id #uuid "89d97203-11d2-57d1-b063-b511b3120ccc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3fbb4c2a-96c4-5c48-b9b8-763a818bbf7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118179" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2{h}^{2}+6h}=\\frac{1}{h+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2h(h+3)}=\\frac{1}{h+3}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(h,\\,2h(h+3),\\,h+3)=2h(h+3).\n$$\n\n- The values $h=0$ and $h=-3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2h(h+3)} & =\\frac{1}{h+3} \\\\ 2h(h+3)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2h(h+3)}) & =2h(h+3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{h+3} \\\\ \\frac{2h(h+3)}{h}-\\frac{2h(h+3)}{2h(h+3)} & =\\frac{2h(h+3)}{h+3} \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{h}\\cdot (h+3)}{\\cancel{h}}-\\frac{\\cancel{2h(h+3)}}{\\cancel{2h(h+3)}} & =\\frac{2h\\cdot \\cancel{(h+3)}}{\\cancel{h+3}} \\\\ 2(h+3)-1 & =2h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $h,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(h+3)-1 & =2h \\\\ 2h+6-1 & =2h \\\\ 5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above resulted in a false statement, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "860736dd-6541-5bbb-96e8-a4ba23d15841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297853} {:answer/id #uuid "78cf6611-96c0-5ba8-a448-e70935807f11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297854} {:answer/id #uuid "e42e44b0-3ce2-5773-a2fc-963979a76fa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297855} {:answer/id #uuid "1501b432-b58a-5c18-9b33-f49d9618bf81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297856} {:answer/id #uuid "fb63d2b4-1741-591d-8422-fb2d69375cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "860736dd-6541-5bbb-96e8-a4ba23d15841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297853} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd200144-71c9-5850-91b8-e607c6c023e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db35d4af-e751-5f8c-ad0f-47acae07432e" :question/math-academy-id "q-14810" :question/problem "Solve the equation$\\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3x+2}-\\frac{2}{x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{1}{(x+1)(x+2)}-\\frac{2}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x+1,\\,x+2,\\,(x+1)(x+2))=(x+1)(x+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $x=-1$ and $x=-2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+2} & =\\frac{1}{(x+1)(x+2)}-\\frac{2}{x+1} \\\\ (x+1)(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2} & =(x+1)(x+2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{(x+1)(x+2)}-\\frac{2}{x+1}) \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)(x+2)}{x+2} & =\\frac{(x+1)(x+2)}{(x+1)(x+2)}-\\frac{2(x+1)(x+2)}{x+1} \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)\\cdot \\cancel{(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x+2}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x+1)(x+2)}}{\\cancel{(x+1)(x+2)}}-\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)}\\cdot (x+2)}{\\cancel{x+1}} \\\\ x+1 & =1-2(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =1-2(x+2) \\\\ x+1 & =1-2x-4 \\\\ 3x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $-\\frac{4}{3}\\ne -1$ and $-\\frac{4}{3}\\ne -2,$ we conclude that $x=-\\frac{4}{3}$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $x=-\\frac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dec0a480-035d-571d-b2b3-405b7246e196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dec0a480-035d-571d-b2b3-405b7246e196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297860} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc9d807b-8a7f-5d54-a3e0-9eccb13e36e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27cd317b-95ca-5aec-b2f6-4fc7eac56306" :question/math-academy-id "q-270927" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{B+2}-\\frac{1}{{B}^{2}+2B}=\\frac{2}{B}.$\n\n$B=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{B+2}-\\frac{1}{B(B+2)}=\\frac{2}{B}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(B+2,\\,B(B+2),\\,B)=B(B+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $B=0$ and $B=-2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{B+2}-\\frac{1}{B(B+2)} & =\\frac{2}{B} \\\\ B(B+2)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{B+2}-\\frac{1}{B(B+2)}) & =B(B+2)\\cdot \\frac{2}{B} \\\\ \\frac{3B(B+2)}{B+2}-\\frac{B(B+2)}{B(B+2)} & =\\frac{2B(B+2)}{B} \\\\ \\frac{3B\\cdot \\cancel{(B+2)}}{\\cancel{B+2}}-\\frac{\\cancel{B(B+2)}}{\\cancel{B(B+2)}} & =\\frac{2\\cancel{B}\\cdot (B+2)}{\\cancel{B}} \\\\ 3B-1 & =2(B+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $B,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3B-1 & =2(B+2) \\\\ B & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5\\ne 0$ and $5\\ne -2,$ we conclude that $B=5$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $B=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97cf25b7-037f-5ad6-848f-07dd0bf94062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297863} {:answer/id #uuid "e96a56e1-e8aa-5257-8c88-6eced71870c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e96a56e1-e8aa-5257-8c88-6eced71870c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297864} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c03050d-fa97-5936-8125-e8909c0397b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "724ac140-4e92-5794-904f-c50fc9c01137" :question/math-academy-id "q-276457" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{3h+2}+\\frac{1}{5h}=\\frac{1}{h(3h+2)}.$\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3h+2,\\,5h,\\,h(3h+2))=5h(3h+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $h=0$ and $h=-\\frac{2}{3}$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3h+2}+\\frac{1}{5h} & =\\frac{1}{h(3h+2)} \\\\ 5h(3h+2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3h+2}+\\frac{1}{5h}) & =5h(3h+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{h(3h+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5h(3h+2)}{3h+2}+\\frac{5h(3h+2)}{5h} & =\\frac{5h(3h+2)}{h(3h+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5h\\cancel{(3h+2)}}{\\cancel{3h+2}}+\\frac{\\cancel{5h}(3h+2)}{\\cancel{5h}} & =\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{h(3h+2)}}{\\cancel{h(3h+2)}} \\\\ 5h+(3h+2) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $h,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5h+(3h+2) & =5 \\\\ 5h+3h+2 & =5 \\\\ 8h & =3 \\\\ h & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{8}\\ne 0$ and $\\frac{3}{8}\\ne -\\frac{2}{3},$ we conclude that $h=\\frac{3}{8}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $h=\\frac{3}{8}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046acada-78e0-5eb9-ade9-a08ac8ee1793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "046acada-78e0-5eb9-ade9-a08ac8ee1793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297867} :answer-field/id #uuid "4006b580-a5cc-52cd-8b89-317238a6fcb2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca42ff57-6263-5e16-bbea-ddcc7f156727" :question/math-academy-id "q-276458" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{1}{3x(x+3)}=\\frac{1}{x+3}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2x,\\,3x(x+3),\\,x+3)=6x(x+3).\n$$\n\n- The values $x=0$ and $x=-3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{1}{3x(x+3)} & =\\frac{1}{x+3} \\\\ 6x(x+3)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{1}{3x(x+3)}) & =6x(x+3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3} \\\\ \\frac{6x(x+3)}{2x}+\\frac{6x(x+3)}{3x(x+3)} & =\\frac{6x(x+3)}{x+3} \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{2x}(x+3)}{\\cancel{2x}}+\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{3x(x+3)}}{\\cancel{3x(x+3)}} & =\\frac{6x\\cancel{(x+3)}}{\\cancel{x+3}} \\\\ 3(x+3)+2 & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+3)+2 & =6x \\\\ 3x+9+2 & =6x \\\\ 11 & =3x \\\\ x & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{11}{3}\\ne 0$ and $\\frac{11}{3}\\ne -3,$ we conclude that $x=\\frac{11}{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $x=\\frac{11}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c54ef1dc-a113-5a55-b347-7a225ee81a3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263471} {:answer/id #uuid "67f3020d-5728-5e8c-9d56-14299c527427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67f3020d-5728-5e8c-9d56-14299c527427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263472} :answer-field/id #uuid "e370c0f2-7b62-53d6-aa27-c9868af45366" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b8e89a0-6a6d-5b9e-b505-b40b9b084524" :question/math-academy-id "q-276459" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)}=\\frac{2}{n}.$\n\n$n=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(n+4,\\,n(n+4),\\,n)=n(n+4).\n$$\n\n- The values $n=-4$ and $n=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)} & =\\frac{2}{n} \\\\ n(n+4)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)}) & =n(n+4)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n} \\\\ \\frac{3n(n+4)}{n+4}-\\frac{5n(n+4)}{n(n+4)} & =\\frac{2n(n+4)}{n} \\\\ \\frac{3n\\cdot \\cancel{(n+4)}}{\\cancel{n+4}}-\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{n(n+4)}}{\\cancel{n(n+4)}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{n}\\cdot (n+4)}{\\cancel{n}} \\\\ 3n-5 & =2(n+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n-5 & =2(n+4) \\\\ 3n-5 & =2n+8 \\\\ n & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $13\\ne 0$ and $13\\ne -4,$ we conclude that $n=13$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $n=13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33fdba8b-2f76-50be-911e-13fae2be6df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33fdba8b-2f76-50be-911e-13fae2be6df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297870} :answer-field/id #uuid "da601fb2-e576-5d03-8969-2a75ecc7797a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "604a1c9f-a782-5971-a297-e70b31599a7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-276460" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{5s(s+1)}+\\frac{3}{5s}=\\frac{1}{s+1}.$\n\n$s=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(5s(s+1),\\,5s,\\,s+1)=5s(s+1).\n$$\n\n- The values $s=0$ and $s=-1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5s(s+1)}+\\frac{3}{5s} & =\\frac{1}{s+1} \\\\ 5s(s+1)\\cdot (\\frac{4}{5s(s+1)}+\\frac{3}{5s}) & =5s(s+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+1} \\\\ \\frac{20s(s+1)}{5s(s+1)}+\\frac{15s(s+1)}{5s} & =\\frac{5s(s+1)}{s+1} \\\\ \\frac{4\\cdot \\cancel{5s(s+1)}}{\\cancel{5s(s+1)}}+\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{5s}(s+1)}{\\cancel{5s}} & =\\frac{5s\\cancel{(s+1)}}{\\cancel{s+1}} \\\\ 4+3(s+1) & =5s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+3(s+1) & =5s \\\\ 4+3s+3 & =5s \\\\ 7 & =2s \\\\ s & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{7}{2}\\ne 0$ and $\\frac{7}{2}\\ne -1,$ we conclude that $s=\\frac{7}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $s=\\frac{7}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af4db8cd-ed49-5ea9-b50e-0d053baab46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263479} {:answer/id #uuid "e14d80a2-0397-58e9-8a76-5749a1dd84d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e14d80a2-0397-58e9-8a76-5749a1dd84d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263480} :answer-field/id #uuid "682de764-2b08-508b-bebb-81cac51c8686" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "561f7a38-14ce-5b18-9a77-8971c0a37b28" :question/math-academy-id "q-276464" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}=\\frac{1}{2{A}^{2}+4A}.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}=\\frac{1}{2A(A+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2A,\\,A+2,\\,2A(A+2))=2A(A+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $A=0$ and $A=-2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2} & =\\frac{1}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ 2A(A+2)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}) & =2A(A+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot 2A(A+2)}{2A}+\\frac{4A(A+2)}{A+2} & =\\frac{2A(A+2)}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{2A}(A+2)}{\\cancel{2A}}+\\frac{4A\\cancel{(A+2)}}{\\cancel{A+2}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2A(A+2)}}{\\cancel{2A(A+2)}} \\\\ 5(A+2)+4A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(A+2)+4A & =1 \\\\ 5A+10+4A & =1 \\\\ 9A & =-9 \\\\ A & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $-1\\ne 0$ and $-1\\ne -2,$ we conclude that $A=-1$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $A=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3ea28c8-8814-5f82-aeab-17ef0ebf07d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297873} {:answer/id #uuid "2b7eaaca-2a2e-5bd1-9f4b-b2480bb312bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3ea28c8-8814-5f82-aeab-17ef0ebf07d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297873} :answer-field/id #uuid "58f40ec9-f503-5bb3-95aa-6c93bc478fb6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb9e4d21-8a7f-5a9f-9b01-4676c8c52014" :question/math-academy-id "q-276549" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{y+2}=\\frac{7}{{y}^{2}-4}-\\frac{1}{y-2}.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{y+2}=\\frac{7}{(y-2)(y+2)}-\\frac{1}{y-2}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(y+2,\\,y-2,\\,(y-2)(y+2))=(y-2)(y+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $y=-2$ and $y=2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{y+2} & =\\frac{7}{(y-2)(y+2)}-\\frac{1}{y-2} \\\\ (y-2)(y+2)\\cdot \\frac{3}{y+2} & =(y-2)(y+2)\\cdot (\\frac{7}{(y-2)(y+2)}-\\frac{1}{y-2}) \\\\ \\frac{3(y-2)(y+2)}{y+2} & =\\frac{7(y-2)(y+2)}{(y-2)(y+2)}-\\frac{(y-2)(y+2)}{y-2} \\\\ \\frac{3(y-2)\\cancel{(y+2)}}{\\cancel{y+2}} & =\\frac{7\\cancel{(y-2)(y+2)}}{\\cancel{(y-2)(y+2)}}-\\frac{\\cancel{(y-2)}(y+2)}{\\cancel{y-2}} \\\\ 3(y-2) & =7-(y+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $y,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(y-2) & =7-(y+2) \\\\ 3y-6 & =7-y-2 \\\\ 4y & =11 \\\\ y & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\frac{11}{4}\\ne -2$ and $\\frac{11}{4}\\ne 2,$ we conclude that $y=\\frac{11}{4}$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $y=\\frac{11}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d5eaaa-518b-5375-a7a6-beced00bb45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14d5eaaa-518b-5375-a7a6-beced00bb45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297877} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cc2cbe0-9f1b-567e-b0ef-ef0fa2d468ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3bbffe4-f265-5a0b-b678-d351a5f34ada" :question/math-academy-id "q-276552" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}-25}=\\frac{1}{w-5}-\\frac{4}{w+5}.$\n\n$w=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(w-5)(w+5)}=\\frac{1}{w-5}-\\frac{4}{w+5}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(w-5,\\,w+5,\\,(w-5)(w+5))=(w-5)(w+5).\n$$\n\n- The values $w=-5$ and $w=5$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(w-5)(w+5)} & =\\frac{1}{w-5}-\\frac{4}{w+5} \\\\ (w-5)(w+5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(w-5)(w+5)} & =(w-5)(w+5)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{w-5}-\\frac{4}{w+5}) \\\\ \\frac{(w-5)(w+5)}{(w-5)(w+5)} & =\\frac{(w-5)(w+5)}{w-5}-\\frac{4(w-5)(w+5)}{x+5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(w-5)(w+5)}}{\\cancel{(w-5)(w+5)}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{(w-5)}(w+5)}{\\cancel{(w-5)}}-\\frac{4(w-5)\\cancel{(w+5)}}{\\cancel{w+5}} \\\\ 1 & =(w+5)-4(w-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $w,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(w+5)-4(w-5) \\\\ 1 & =w+5-4w+20 \\\\ 3w & =24 \\\\ w & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $8\\ne -5$ and $8\\ne 5,$ we conclude that $w=8$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $w=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8e3811e-e9ca-58a7-b7d1-1c02489638fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080799} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd21c24-db32-59e3-b97a-a8e1d54f6b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080800} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8764f1-0c56-5c07-9313-2d8c2be4412d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080801} {:answer/id #uuid "284baadb-f901-5d5a-af8e-e4c2cd289eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080802} {:answer/id #uuid "33d837ea-eed9-5d9d-a23e-6343ec4b04fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z =-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbd21c24-db32-59e3-b97a-a8e1d54f6b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080800} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a71dd97-0aea-5082-94ed-688939cf60f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22844bb0-085b-5dec-9770-a4294665f723" :question/math-academy-id "q-3519" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{z-1}-\\frac{3}{z(z-1)}=\\frac{2}{z}.$" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(z,\\,z-1,\\,z(z-1))=z(z-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $z=0$ and $z=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{z-1}-\\frac{3}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{2}{z} \\\\ z(z-1)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{z-1}-\\frac{3}{z(z-1)}) & =z(z-1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{z} \\\\ \\frac{3z(z-1)}{z-1}-\\frac{3z(z-1)}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{2z(z-1)}{z} \\\\ \\frac{3z\\cdot \\cancel{(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z-1}}-\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{z(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z(z-1)}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{z}\\cdot (z-1)}{\\cancel{z}} \\\\ 3z-3 & =2z-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $z,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z-3 & =2z-2 \\\\ z & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $z=1$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}]}
